// ============================================================================
// Error Analysis — Finding and Diagnosing Problems
// ============================================================================
//
// WHAT YOU WILL LEARN:
//   - How to find errors in AppExceptions
//   - How to correlate errors with the requests that caused them
//   - How to find failed model calls and understand why they failed
//   - How to use "contains" and "startswith" for text searching
//   - How to group errors to find the most common problems
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS:
//   When your Foundry agents fail, the evidence is in the logs.
//   Knowing how to quickly find and diagnose errors saves hours of debugging.
//
// WHERE TO RUN:
//   Azure Portal → Log Analytics workspace → Logs
// ============================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Are there any recent exceptions?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's check if any errors have been recorded.

AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| count

// If the count is 0, your application has been running error-free.
// If the count is > 0, let's investigate.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Browse recent exceptions
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| project TimeGenerated, ExceptionType, OuterMessage, ProblemId, SeverityLevel
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
| take 20

// Key columns:
//   ExceptionType  → The error class (e.g., "openai.APIError", "HttpRequestError")
//   OuterMessage   → Human-readable error description
//   ProblemId      → A grouping key to find similar errors
//   SeverityLevel  → How critical (3 = Error, 4 = Critical)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: What are the most common error types?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Group exceptions by type to see patterns.

AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| summarize ErrorCount = count() by ExceptionType
| sort by ErrorCount desc

// The most frequent error type appears at the top.
// Common ones you might see with Foundry:
//   "openai.RateLimitError"    → Too many requests to the model
//   "openai.APIError"          → General API error
//   "HttpRequestError"         → Network connectivity issue
//   "openai.AuthenticationError" → Invalid API key or credentials


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Group errors by ProblemId
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ProblemId groups similar errors together, even if the messages differ slightly.

AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| summarize
    ErrorCount     = count(),
    FirstSeen      = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen       = max(TimeGenerated),
    SampleMessage  = take_any(OuterMessage)
    by ProblemId, ExceptionType
| sort by ErrorCount desc

// "take_any()" grabs one example Message from the group.
// This gives you a quick overview of all distinct problems.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Find failed dependency calls (model call failures)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Not all errors are exceptions. Model calls can fail with HTTP error codes.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Success == false
| project TimeGenerated, Name, Target, ResultCode, DurationMs
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
| take 20

// Common ResultCode values:
//   "429" → Rate limited — you're sending too many requests
//   "401" → Unauthorized — check your credentials
//   "403" → Forbidden — check your permissions
//   "500" → Internal server error — issue on Azure's side
//   "503" → Service unavailable — temporary outage


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Count failed calls by result code
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where Success == false
| summarize FailureCount = count() by ResultCode
| sort by FailureCount desc
| render columnchart

// The bar chart quickly shows which error codes are most common.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Failed model calls specifically
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Filter to only AI model call failures.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where Success == false
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend
    ModelUsed  = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.request.model"]),
    Operation  = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.operation.name"])
| summarize FailureCount = count() by ModelUsed, ResultCode
| sort by FailureCount desc

// This tells you which models are failing and why.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Success rate over time
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What percentage of model calls succeed each hour?

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| summarize
    TotalCalls   = count(),
    SuccessCalls = countif(Success == true),
    FailedCalls  = countif(Success == false)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)
| extend SuccessRate = round(100.0 * SuccessCalls / TotalCalls, 1)
| project TimeGenerated, TotalCalls, SuccessCalls, FailedCalls, SuccessRate
| sort by TimeGenerated asc

// "countif()" counts only rows matching a condition — very useful!
// A healthy application should show a SuccessRate near 100%.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Visualize success rate over time
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| summarize
    TotalCalls   = count(),
    SuccessCalls = countif(Success == true)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)
| extend SuccessRate = round(100.0 * SuccessCalls / TotalCalls, 1)
| project TimeGenerated, SuccessRate
| render timechart

// A sudden drop in the success rate chart is an immediate red flag.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Correlate an exception with its full trace
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// When you find an exception, use its OperationId to see the full context.

// First, find an exception and its OperationId:
AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| project TimeGenerated, ExceptionType, OuterMessage, OperationId
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
| take 5

// Then use that OperationId to see everything that happened:
// (Replace with an actual OperationId from the query above)

let traceId = "PASTE_OPERATION_ID_FROM_EXCEPTION";
union
    (AppRequests     | where OperationId == traceId | project TimeGenerated, Type = "Request",    Info = Name, Success),
    (AppDependencies | where OperationId == traceId | project TimeGenerated, Type = "Dependency", Info = Name, Success),
    (AppExceptions   | where OperationId == traceId | project TimeGenerated, Type = "Exception",  Info = strcat(ExceptionType, ": ", OuterMessage), Success = false),
    (AppTraces       | where OperationId == traceId | project TimeGenerated, Type = "Trace",      Info = Message, Success = true)
| sort by TimeGenerated asc

// This gives you the full timeline of what happened leading up to the error.
// You can see exactly which step failed and what came before it.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Search for specific error messages
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Use "contains" to search within text columns.

AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where OuterMessage contains "rate limit"
| project TimeGenerated, ExceptionType, OuterMessage
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
| take 10

// "contains" does a case-insensitive substring search.
// Use "startswith" if you know how the message begins:
//   | where OuterMessage startswith "Error"
// Use "has" for whole-word matching (faster than contains):
//   | where OuterMessage has "timeout"


// ============================================================================
// RECAP — What You Learned:
//
//   - AppExceptions     → Stores exception/error records
//   - Success == false  → Finds failed dependency calls in AppDependencies
//   - countif()         → Counts rows matching a condition
//   - contains          → Case-insensitive substring search
//   - startswith        → Match the beginning of a string
//   - has               → Whole-word search (faster)
//   - strcat()          → Concatenate strings together
//   - take_any()        → Grab one example value from a group
//
//   Combining AppExceptions + AppDependencies gives you a complete
//   error analysis toolkit for your Foundry agents.
//
// NEXT: Build monitoring dashboard queries.
// ============================================================================
